#. Tag: title
#: notrelated.docbook:10
#, no-c-format
msgid "Not really &kde;-related, but frequently asked nevertheless."
msgstr " "

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:15
#, no-c-format
msgid "How do I change the screen resolution?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:18
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Use <keycombo action=\"simul\">&Ctrl;&Alt;<keycap>+</keycap></keycombo> "
"and <keycombo action=\"simul\">&Ctrl;&Alt;<keycap>-</keycap></keycombo> "
"to cycle through the resolutions you have defined in <filename>"
"XF86Config</filename> (maybe under <filename class=\"directory\">"
"/etc/X11</filename>; poke around first). If you prefer to get your hands dirty, "
"you can always edit the file directly. By placing your favorite resolution at "
"the beginning (or making it the only one listed), &X-Window; will always start "
"up with that resolution."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:27
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Always make a backup copy of your <filename>XF86Config</filename> "
"file <emphasis>before</emphasis> you start editing it. Errors in this file can "
"render &X-Window; unusable."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:30
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"These instructions are only valid if you are running version 3.3.x of the "
"XFree86 server. If you are running XFree86 4.x, you must consult with the "
"<ulink url=\"http://xfree.org\">XFree86(TM): Home Page</ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:39
#, no-c-format
msgid "How do I change the color depth?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:42
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There is no way you can do this on the fly. You can either start &X-Window; "
"using <command>startx <option> -bpp <replaceable>number</replaceable></option>"
"</command> where <replaceable>number</replaceable> can be 8, 16, 24 or 32, "
"depending on the depth you want. Alternatively, if you are using <application>"
"xdm</application>/&kdm;, you need to edit <filename>"
"/etc/X11/xdm/Xservers</filename> (may vary) and enter <userinput>"
":0 local /usr/X11R6/bin/X -bpp 16</userinput> for 16 bit color depth."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:52
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can also edit the <filename>XF86Config</filename> "
"file and add a line like <userinput>DefaultColorDepth <replaceable>"
"number</replaceable></userinput> to Section \"Screen\". The next time you start "
"X, it will run with the newly-configured color depth."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:62
#, no-c-format
msgid "What can I do if I am using a 2-button mouse?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:65
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Go buy a 3-button one, or use third button emulation. The third button is "
"emulated by pressing both the &LMB; and the &RMB; together. You would need to "
"enable <option>Emulate3Buttons</option> in your <filename>XF86Config</filename> "
"file."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:74
#, no-c-format
msgid "What is a \"sticky\" window?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:77
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In a virtual desktop environment with multiple virtual desktops, a \"sticky\" "
"window will stay put on the screen when you switch between desktops, as if "
"sticking to the glass of the screen. <application>Xclock</application> "
"is a typical candidate for sticking, as you need to run only one instance of "
"it, and it always stays with you."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:88
#, no-c-format
msgid "How do I replace the \"X\" mouse pointer with an arrow?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:91
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The various types of cursor available in X are defined in <filename "
"class=\"headerfile\">X11/cursorfont.h</filename>. You can change it using "
"<command>xsetroot -cursor_name <replaceable>name_of_cursor</replaceable>"
"</command>. For example, I have the following in my <filename>"
".Xclients</filename>:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: notrelated.docbook:96
#, no-c-format
msgid "xsetroot -cursor_name left_ptr"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:97
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This will create the common left-angled pointer. To see other alternatives, "
"type <command>xfd -fn cursor</command>. And of course, do not forget that "
"<command>man xsetroot</command> is your friend."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:106
#, no-c-format
msgid "How do I extract/install diff files?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:109
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To generate a context-diff suitable for patching, use <command>"
"diff -u <replaceable>old-file new-file</replaceable> &gt; <replaceable>"
"patchfile</replaceable></command>. To apply the diff to a file (<abbrev>"
"i.e.</abbrev> \"patch the file\"), execute <command>patch &lt; <replaceable>"
"patchfile</replaceable></command>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:119
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"How do I get &Linux; to mount the floppy device for use with both DOS and ext2 "
"formatted floppies?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:123
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Just specify the filesystem type as auto in <filename>/etc/fstab</filename>"
". Auto detection works fine for DOS and ext2."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:131
#, no-c-format
msgid "How do I start &kde; with the <keycap>Num Lock</keycap> on?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:134
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Have you tried <command>man setleds</command>? In addition, you might want to "
"edit your <filename>$<envar>HOME</envar>/.Xmodmap</filename> "
"and put the following lines in:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: notrelated.docbook:138
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"! redefines numeric keypad to be used without NumLock\n"
"keycode 79 = 7\n"
"keycode 80 = 8\n"
"keycode 81 = 9\n"
"\n"
"keycode 83 = 4\n"
"keycode 84 = 5\n"
"keycode 85 = 6\n"
"\n"
"keycode 87 = 1\n"
"keycode 88 = 2\n"
"keycode 89 = 3\n"
"\n"
"keycode 90 = 0\n"
"keycode 91 = comma\n"
"keycode 86 = plus\n"
"\n"
"! deactivates NumLock key\n"
"keycode 77 ="
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:139
#, no-c-format
msgid "Other possible alternatives:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:141
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<application>xkeycaps</application>: <mousebutton>right</mousebutton> "
"clicking should allow edits. You may have to do this as root."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:144
#, no-c-format
msgid "<command>man XF86Config</command> and look under section Keyboard."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:146
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Install <application>NumLockX</application>, which is available from <ulink "
"url=\"http://dforce.sh.cvut.cz/~seli/en/numlockx\">http://dforce.sh.cvut.cz/~sel"
"i/en/numlockx</ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:155
#, no-c-format
msgid "How do I take window or desktop screenshots?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:158
#, no-c-format
msgid "Use &ksnapshot;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:164
#, no-c-format
msgid "Is there a tool to make webpages?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:167
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Yes, and there are a lot of them out there, including <application>"
"StarOffice</application>, <application>&Netscape; Composer</application>"
", and <application>XEmacs</application>. There are also many &kde; "
"applications. To find the most current list, go to <ulink "
"url=\"http://kde-apps.org\">kde-apps.org: The Latest in &kde; "
"Applications</ulink> and search for <emphasis>web development</emphasis>"
". Try as many as possible and choose the one most suitable to your needs."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: notrelated.docbook:180
#, no-c-format
msgid "What do all those acronyms like AFAIK mean?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: notrelated.docbook:183
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"AAMOF: as a matter of fact\n"
"AFAIK: as far as I know\n"
"AISE: as I see it\n"
"BFN: bye for now\n"
"BION: believe it or not\n"
"BRB: be right back\n"
"BTW: by the way\n"
"CMIIW: correct me if I am wrong\n"
"FUD: fear, uncertainty, and doubt\n"
"FWIW: for what it's worth\n"
"FYI: for your information\n"
"HTH: hope this helps\n"
"IIRC: if I recall correctly\n"
"IMHO: in my humble opinion\n"
"LOL: laughing out loud\n"
"MYOB: mind your own business\n"
"PITA: pain in the ass\n"
"ROTFL: rolling on the floor laughing\n"
"RTFM: read the fine manual\n"
"SOP: standard operating procedure\n"
"TIA: thanks in advance\n"
"YMMV: your mileage may vary"
msgstr ""

